## What Claude Code did most recently (June 16 2026)

### LPL / Cetera landing pages — bottom CTA restyled to match the sitewide CTABanner pattern

- **PartnerLandingPage.jsx** — the bottom CTA was a bespoke full-bleed navy section with two buttons, the only page on the site built that way. Every other "bottom CTA" (homepage, ProductDetailPage, CompanyPage, InsightsPage, ProfessionalsPage, etc.) uses the shared `CTABanner` component: a single contained, rounded navy card sitting inside `ov-container` on a white section background. Replaced the bespoke markup with `<CTABanner eyebrow title titleAccent body cta onClick>`, dropping the second "Browse Sales Tools" button (homepage's CTABanner usage is single-button too) and removing the now-unused `secondaryLabel` field from the `cta` data in `LPLLandingPage.jsx`/`CeteraLandingPage.jsx`. Fixes both pages since they share this component.
- (Earlier in this same investigation, briefly fixed just the "Browse Sales Tools" destination to scroll to the in-page resources section instead of navigating to `#sales-tools` — superseded by this restyle, which removes that button entirely.)

### LPL / Cetera landing pages — bottom CTA's "Browse Sales Tools" pointed to the wrong place

- **PartnerLandingPage.jsx** — the bottom CTA's secondary button navigated to the generic, non-partner-specific `#sales-tools` page, abandoning the partner-specific resources already built into the same page (rate sheets, wholesaler maps, etc. in the `resources` section). Changed it to scroll to `resources.sectionId` instead, matching the pattern the hero's own secondary button already used. Fixes both LPL and Cetera since they share this component.

### LPL / Cetera landing pages — button sizing/overflow fixes

- **PartnerLandingPage.jsx** case-study CTA ("Read Their Story") — sat in a `flexDirection:'column'` container with default `align-items:stretch`, stretching the pill button to the full column width instead of sizing to its content. Added `alignSelf:'flex-start'` to the button.
- **PartnerLandingPage.jsx** bottom CTA buttons ("Contact Us" / "Browse Sales Tools") — the wrapper had `flexShrink:0`, which meant it never shrank enough on mobile to trigger its own `flexWrap:wrap`, so the second button overflowed off the right edge of the screen instead of stacking. Removed `flexShrink:0`, added `maxWidth:'100%'` — buttons now wrap to full-width stacked rows on mobile and stay side-by-side on desktop.
- Both fixes apply to LPL and Cetera simultaneously since they share this one component.

### LPL / Cetera landing page heroes — same scrim bug as ProductDetailPage

- **PartnerLandingPage.jsx** (shared hero/layout for both `LPLLandingPage.jsx` and `CeteraLandingPage.jsx`) — had the exact same two-part bug fixed earlier on `ProductDetailPage.jsx`: the scrim gradient started with `rgba(0,0,0,.82)` (black) instead of `rgba(0,31,84,.82)` (navy), and the scrim div was missing the `ov-hero-scrim` className so it never got the mobile flat-scrim override. Fixed both — one file fixes both partner pages since they share this component.

### Professionals page — added a link to the LPL partner landing page

- **ProfessionalsPage.jsx** — `#lpl-landing` (and `#cetera-landing`) were real, working routes with nothing on the site linking to them. Added a 7th card ("LPL Advisor Resources") to the "Advisor Resources" portal grid linking to `#lpl-landing`. Cetera's equivalent page is still unlinked — only LPL was requested.

### Legal pages — "LEGAL" eyebrow was flush left instead of centered

- **LegalPage.jsx** (shared shell for Privacy, Terms, Disclaimers, Accessibility) — the hero section has `textAlign:'center'`, but the shared `Eyebrow` component (`common.jsx`) renders a `display:'flex'` div, and `textAlign` has no effect on flex containers (only on inline content) — so the eyebrow row stayed pinned to the left edge while the h1/subtitle (plain text, which `textAlign` does affect) centered correctly. Fixed by passing `style={{ justifyContent: 'center' }}` into `<Eyebrow>` — `Eyebrow` already spreads its `style` prop onto the flex container. One-line fix in the shared shell fixes all 4 pages at once.

### Newsroom hero fix + tabs upgraded to match Contact/Client Resources pattern; Leadership card padding; back-link on article page

- **NewsroomPage.jsx** — hero image was `assets/hero-overlay.jpg`, which (per the earlier discovery on the Our Story timeline) is a plain noise/grain texture, not a photo — so the hero rendered with no real image. Swapped to `assets/two.jpg`.
- **NewsroomPage.jsx** — replaced the generic underline-style `TabBar.jsx` category filter (no URL persistence) with the same sticky full-width segmented tab pattern already established on `ContactPage.jsx` (equal-width flex tabs, light-teal active background `rgba(226,241,242,.6)`, right-border separators, ARIA `role="tablist"`/`role="tab"` with arrow/home/end keyboard nav, reuses the shared `.ov-contact-tab` focus-visible CSS). Category ids are now kebab-case (`press-releases`, etc.) and the active tab persists via `history.replaceState` to `#newsroom?tab=<id>`, restored on load — same convention as Contact and Client Resources. Removed the now-unused `TabBar.jsx` import from this page — the component itself is still used by `ProductsCard.jsx`, so it stays.
- **LeadershipPage.jsx** — team card name/role/bio sat with zero horizontal padding, flush against the card edges (only the photo had its own rounded inset). Wrapped name/role/bio in a `cardBody` div with `padding: '0 6px 8px'`.
- **BlogArticlePage.jsx** — added a "← Back to Blog" link above the eyebrow, linking to `#blog`. The article page was otherwise a dead end with no way back to the listing except the browser back button.

### Our Story timeline — moved images to the left, centered the block

- **OurStoryPage.jsx** — reordered each timeline row from [line+dot][text][image] to [image][line+dot][text]. The connector line moved from the outer wrapper's left padding edge to a fixed offset (`TL_IMG_W + TL_GAP` = 252px) so it sits between the image column and the text; the dot moved from `left:-36` (relative to the text block, compensating for the old 32px outer padding) to `left:-4.5` (centered directly on the new line position, relative to the text block's own edge — simpler now that the indent math isn't split across two nested paddings).
- Capped the whole timeline block at `maxWidth: 840` with `margin: '0 auto'` so it's centered within the (wider) page container instead of stretching edge-to-edge.
- Bug caught after first pass: below ~720px the image wraps above the text (flex-wrap), but the line is absolutely positioned at a fixed offset from the *outer* wrapper, so once wrapped it no longer lines up with the dot and floats orphaned. Fixed by adding `.ov-story-timeline-line` and hiding it via a new `@media (max-width: 720px)` rule in tokens.css — the dots alone still read fine as markers once stacked.

### Our Story timeline — added a thumbnail image per milestone

- **OurStoryPage.jsx** — the 7-milestone vertical timeline was text-only. Added an `img` field to each `MILESTONES` entry and updated `Timeline()` to render a 220px rounded thumbnail (object-cover, 4:3) alongside each entry's text, wrapping below the text on narrow viewports via `flexWrap` (no new CSS needed — same flex-basis + wrap pattern already used elsewhere on this page, e.g. `ov-split-row`).
- Picked varied existing assets per era (lighthouse.jpg for 1987 founding, couple-walking.png, hero-beach-couple.jpg, four.jpg, older-couple-1.png, the literal `ambest.png` A (Excellent) badge for the 2021 rating milestone, family.png for "Today"). Initially tried `hero-overlay.jpg` for the "Today" entry — turned out to be a plain noise/grain texture file, not a photo (good reminder to actually open image assets before using them, not just trust the filename). Swapped to `family.png`.

### New page: default blog article template (`#blog-annuity-timing`)

- **BlogArticlePage.jsx** — new, built from Figma node `7391-3634` ("Blog Article"). The Figma file used outdated/inconsistent fonts (Inter for body, Playfair Display for subheadings, Cormorant Garamond in the footer) and a one-off teal CTA card not used anywhere else on the live site. Rebuilt with this build's actual tokens instead: `var(--ov-ff-display)` (PP Editorial New) for h1/h3, `var(--ov-ff-sans)` (PP Mori) for body, navy `#233D7C`/`#0D1F4E` and grey `#4A5568`/`#6B7280` from the existing palette. Structure: eyebrow + h1 + date, intro paragraphs, full-width rounded hero image, two H3 sections with body + bullet lists, a `DownloadRow` resource card (same pattern as `ProductDetailPage`'s), a sticky navy sidebar CTA card (same pattern as `ProductDetailPage`'s `pdt-sidebar-cta`), and a bottom `CTABanner` (same component every other page already uses) instead of Figma's mismatched teal card.
- **tokens.css** — added `.ov-article-layout` / `.ov-article-sidebar` responsive rules (≤900px: stack to one column, sidebar CTA goes static full-width instead of sticky) — same pattern as `.pdt-layout`.
- **Page.jsx** — wired route `blog-annuity-timing` (import, switch case, `PAGE_ROUTES`, `ROUTE_TO_NAV` → "Blog", page title + meta description).
- **BlogPage.jsx** — added a real "Education" category post card linking to `#blog-annuity-timing` so the new template is reachable from the blog listing's "Read more" link, not just a dangling route.
- This is a single hardcoded page (not a generic per-article template/data system) — matches the existing convention where most content pages are hardcoded components, reserving the "shared template + per-page data" pattern (like `ProductDetailPage`) for cases with many near-identical instances.
- **InsightsPage.jsx** — of the 6 "Read →" article cards in the "All Insights" grid, 4 were standalone-article cards with no real dedicated page (they pointed back to a hub page as a placeholder: "Sequence-of-Returns Risk", "Navigating Divorce and Retirement Assets", "Fixed Annuities vs. CDs", "Longevity Risk and the Case for Lifetime Income"). Repointed those 4 to `#blog-annuity-timing`. Left "Life Events Series" and "White Papers" pointing at their real, already-built hub pages (`#life-events`, `#white-papers`) since those are correct as-is.

### Contact page — gap between header CTA and sticky tab nav

- **ContactPage.jsx** — page header section had `padding: 'clamp(72px,9vw,112px) 0 0'` (top only, zero bottom), so the "Send us a message" button sat flush against the sticky tab nav below. Changed to `'...0 48px'` for breathing room.

### Product detail hero polish + mobile section-nav "tabs" made more obvious

- **ProductDetailPage.jsx** — hero's category badge (e.g. "MYGA") used `backdropFilter: 'blur(4px)'`, the only CSS feature in this hero that diverges from `PageHero`/`Hero` (which don't use backdrop-filter at all). Backdrop blur is a common point of failure for HTML-to-design import tools. Removed it, bumped the badge background to a solid `rgba(0,31,84,.45)` so it still reads clearly against the scrim without needing blur.
- **tokens.css** — the mobile sticky section-nav (`.pdt-sidebar-nav`, the horizontal pill bar that replaces the desktop sidebar on ≤900px) had `margin-bottom: 0`, butting directly against the next section. Added `margin-bottom: 24px` to `.pdt-layout > aside` on mobile for breathing room. Also made the pills themselves much more obviously tab-like: added a visible border to inactive pills (previously near-invisible `rgba(13,31,78,.05)` fill with no border), and changed the active state from a light teal tint to a solid `#2494C1` fill with white text + shadow so the selected section is unmistakable. Added a right-edge mask-image fade on the scroll container to hint there are more tabs to scroll to.
- Applies to all 7 product detail pages (Harbourview MYGA, Horizon MYGA, Sky Harbourview MYGA, Current Rate FIA, Harbourview FIA, CapLock, Topsider) since they share `ProductDetailPage.jsx` and the global `tokens.css` rules.
- Stats bar (navy $20K/3–10/10%/1%/A-rating row) had only `padding: '12px 0'`, butting almost directly against the tinted content section below. Bumped to `'12px 0 40px'` for clear separation.

### Footer newsletter signup — removed <form> wrapper (Figma HTML import was dropping it)

- **Footer.jsx** — the newsletter signup (name/email inputs, checkbox, Get Started button) was wrapped in a real `<form>` element. HTML-to-design importers (Figma's HTML import and similar plugins) skip `<form>` nodes when walking the DOM since forms have no design-tool equivalent, which silently dropped the entire signup block on import. Replaced `<form>` with a `<div role="form" aria-label="Newsletter signup">`; moved submit logic from `onSubmit` to the button's `onClick` (`submitNewsletter`), added refs on the two inputs and `reportValidity()` checks to preserve native required-field validation, and added an Enter-key handler (`handleEnterKey`) on both inputs so pressing Enter still submits like a real form would. Verified via Playwright: 0 `<form>` elements remain in the footer, and click-to-submit, empty-field validation, and Enter-key submission all still work.

### Product detail page hero — missing mobile scrim treatment + wrong scrim color

- **ProductDetailPage.jsx** (shared template for all 7 product pages — Harbourview MYGA, Horizon MYGA, Sky Harbourview MYGA, Current Rate FIA, Harbourview FIA, CapLock, Topsider) — its hero gradient overlay div was missing the `ov-hero-scrim` className, so it never got the mobile flat-scrim override (`rgba(0,31,84,.65)` at ≤720px, see `tokens.css`) that `PageHero.jsx`/`Hero.jsx` already had. Added the className; verified via Playwright at 390px that the computed background now correctly flips to the flat mobile scrim.
- Same file — the gradient itself started with `rgba(0,0,0,.82)` (black) instead of `rgba(0,31,84,.82)` (navy) like `PageHero`/`Hero` use, making product page heroes look gray/washed-out instead of the rich navy tint seen on pages like About. Fixed to match.

### Mobile footer overflow fix

- **Footer.jsx** — `S.signupRow` and `S.linkSection` were `display:'flex'` inline but `tokens.css` media queries tried to override them with `grid-template-columns`, which has no effect on a flex container — so the nav columns (Products/Company/Client Resources/Insights) never stacked on mobile and overflowed ~175px past the viewport edge. Converted both to `display:'grid'` (`signupRow`: `488px 538px` cols; `linkSection`: `1.2fr 1fr 1fr 1fr 1fr` cols) so the existing breakpoint overrides at 960/800/480px now actually apply. Also changed `signupLeft`/`signupRight` from fixed `width:488/538` to `maxWidth` + `width:'100%'`, and `S.input` from fixed `width:171` to `width:'100%'`, so they shrink on narrow screens instead of forcing horizontal overflow. Removed now-unused `flex:1.2`/`flex:1` from `logoCol`/`linkCol` (grid ignores the flex property).
- **Footer.jsx / tokens.css** — `copyrightRow` (copyright text + 5 legal links) had no mobile handling at all and would overflow on phones. Added `ov-footer-legal-row` / `ov-footer-legal-links` classNames with a new `@media (max-width: 640px)` rule in `tokens.css` that stacks copyright above the links and lets the links wrap.
- Verified via Playwright at 390×844 (iPhone 12): `document.body.scrollWidth` went from 565px (overflowing) to 390px (no overflow) after the fix; desktop (1440px) screenshot confirmed unchanged.
- Root cause was also visible in the Figma mobile mockup itself (node `7662-2510`) — the link columns overflow the 390px frame there too, confirming the fix direction (stack to single column) rather than trying to replicate the Figma frame's overflow.

